So the issue here is that when calling refresh(), you cannot alter the query that runs beneath. This is not relatable to the query itself, but the modifiers like pessimistic locking:

```php
// Bad
DB::transaction(function () {
    $model->refresh();

   // Do something with the model that requires deadlock.
});

// Better
DB::transaction(function () {
    $model->refresh(fn ($query) => $query->lockForUpdate());

   // Do something with the model that requires deadlock.
});
```

This specific use case is relatable to the fact we can run database transactions that use deadlocks, but if we want to refresh the model in a function within itself, running refresh will not lock correctly the model until we are finished with it.

The only alternative I had was to define the following code in some model method and use it in the transaction, but it looks kinda ugly.

```php
public function refreshWithLock()
{
    $this->lockForUpdate()->find($this->id);
    $this->refresh();
}
```

```php
DB::transaction(function () {
    $model->refreshWithLock();

   // Do something with the model that requires deadlock.
});
```

